WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:09.200
So, context engineering, possibly the
the single most popular expression in in

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:14.480
our world last year. Uh, context
engineering. So, look, the the the

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:19.680
output of an LLM, we know it's based
entirely on the input. It's stateless.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:23.760
You give it an input. It produces an
output, the most likely tokens to follow

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:27.760
that input. And that input is sometimes
known as the context because it's all

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:32.960
the contextual information that we're
giving the LLM that it can use to

00:00:29.599 --> 00:00:37.760
generate the output. Getting that input
right, getting the context right, that's

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:41.360
all we've got. That's it. The output is
only based on the input. So to say

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.200
getting this right is everything. It's
it's almost like obvious that of course

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:51.280
it's everything. That's all it has. It's
got that input. And so getting it right

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:54.879
is what this game is all about. We used
to call that prompt engineering.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:58.480
Remember when prompt engineering a
couple years ago was with like like a

00:00:56.399 --> 00:01:02.239
job that people could have and it was
the idea that you had to figure out the

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:06.799
best way to prompt an LLM. But really
that term prompt engineering has evolved

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:10.960
into this bigger term context
engineering which is recognizing it's

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:15.280
not just about the prompt. It's all
other things like the kind the tools you

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:20.640
give it and and other things that that
surround it to make sure that you are

00:01:17.439 --> 00:01:24.960
doing the best job you possibly can do
to give the LLM all the right

00:01:22.799 --> 00:01:30.799
information in the context so that the
output is most likely to achieve your

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:34.320
goal that that whole set of techniques
is what's known as context engineering

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:40.400
and there are a lot of different
components of what goes into this input

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:43.680
context this input to an LLM Um, first
of them, the the first thing and and one

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:47.200
of the first things you you learn about
when you're when you're calling an Alan

00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:51.200
directly is there's a part of the
prompt, part of the text input that's

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:56.799
known as the system prompt that is the
most crucial, the the most general

00:01:54.079 --> 00:02:01.680
information that frames the the overall
situation. What what role is this LLM

00:01:59.759 --> 00:02:06.079
playing? What's its what's its overall
job? Is it an agent that's responsible

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:11.599
for writing code? Is it an agent that is
a customer support agent for an airline?

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:15.280
Whatever it is, that overall framing,
including the tone it should take and

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:19.200
the approach it should use, that goes
into something called the system prompt,

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:24.480
which usually is at the very beginning
of the information that is sent to the

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:28.400
LLM. There's then a set of descriptions
of the different tools that it has

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:32.560
access to. A bit like the way I said
that that if it put the word Python and

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:36.959
then something, it would be saying, I
want to run that in Python. and that

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:41.760
descriptions of the different tools,
that's also part of the the the context.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:45.360
That's what might go in there next. And
of course, the more tools you want it to

00:02:43.519 --> 00:02:49.360
have access to, the more space you will
fill up with these tools. And and some

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:52.800
people might say that that those tool
descriptions are actually part of the

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:55.599
system prompt. And that's really a
definitional thing. It doesn't

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:59.599
particularly matter if you think of them
as being part of the same thing or or

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:03.519
just two separate chunks. It's more just
about how you label it. But there in for

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:08.640
this diagram anyway I'm saying there is
the overall framing followed by the

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:13.920
tools and then there's something that is
sometimes called the memory. And this is

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:18.800
basically information that you want to
persist across potentially multiple

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:22.400
different conversations. Some people
call this long-term memory or

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:27.280
medium-term memory, but this is
typically general resources that can

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:32.799
change over time, but that you'd want to
always be able to come back to. And then

00:03:30.159 --> 00:03:38.720
there is the big part of it which is
basically the the the actual input that

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:43.840
you're sending to the LLM. But remember
because of the this way that we have

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:48.959
this this trick that that allows us to
give the illusion of a memory. What we

00:03:46.239 --> 00:03:53.120
send can't just be the most recent
message that we want to send the LLM.

00:03:50.879 --> 00:03:57.599
It's got to be the entire conversation
so far. It's got to be the very first

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:01.840
message that we sent and then its reply
and then our follow- on message and its

00:03:59.519 --> 00:04:07.360
reply to that. So all of the messages
that formed this conversation to the LLM

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:11.360
all need to fit next in the context.
They all need to be squeezed in next.

00:04:09.439 --> 00:04:15.920
And that also needs to include any
reasoning that the LLM has done, any of

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:19.359
the these tokens that it generates to
describe its thought process. There are

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.240
there are sometimes when you you can
actually not not include that, but

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:26.880
generally speaking, that does tend to
get included here. and it's it's replies

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:31.199
and then if it's generated code then
that code that it's generated needs to

00:04:28.639 --> 00:04:35.520
go here. If it's called tools then the
tools it's decided to call and the

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:40.639
output from calling those tools that all
has to go in here. All this information

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:45.680
forms the input and when it's generating
the output it will be generating output

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:51.360
that's consistent with this entire
conversation history of the full

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:56.639
conversation so far. So all of that
makes up the single input to the LLM

00:04:54.720 --> 00:05:01.919
because it's stateless. It needs to get
this entire package that you see here in

00:04:59.919 --> 00:05:05.759
order to be able to generate the the
most likely next token that will be

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:10.080
consistent with our objective. And
there's something else in the coding

00:05:08.479 --> 00:05:14.479
agents that we're going to be working
with. There is a special thing that gets

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:18.479
included in there too which is really I
guess part of memory but I'm going to

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:24.080
show it as a separate separate line item
if you will a separate thing that gets

00:05:20.479 --> 00:05:29.840
shoved in here and it is a special file
and it's often called agents.md

00:05:27.919 --> 00:05:32.400
and you may well know about this already
but we'll be looking at it and we'll be

00:05:31.199 --> 00:05:36.240
talking a lot about what makes a good
one and a bad one and so on and you can

00:05:34.479 --> 00:05:39.520
think of this as being like part of the
memory it's it's a resource that

00:05:37.919 --> 00:05:43.039
persists or you can say this is
something something extra RA in addition

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:47.840
to the other parts of memory. This
agents.m MD is something special. And of

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:53.280
course, if you're working with claude
code, it's in fact claude.md. And with

00:05:50.479 --> 00:05:58.720
anti-gravity, it's gemini.md. But these
are all what we generally think of as

00:05:54.720 --> 00:06:04.240
the agents.md files. And these are files
that we particularly use with coding

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:08.080
agents that describe special things
about the project we're working on,

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:12.560
particular coding standards or things
about our objective, something that we

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:16.639
want to be in the memory of the AI agent
while it's writing code for us. That is

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:22.160
agents.md,
a special file we write that gets shoved

00:06:19.199 --> 00:06:25.440
in the context every single time. Ah, I
can hear you correcting me there. You

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:28.639
know that it's not every single time.
It's more complicated than that. But

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:32.800
never fear, we will get to that. We will
get to that. But for now, consider it to

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:38.160
be every single time. All right. So,
that is the context. That's what context

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:41.919
engineering is all about. And as you
probably also know, but there's no harm

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:50.319
in us going through it again. There is
of course this idea of a context window

00:06:45.919 --> 00:06:57.199
which is to say that an LLM has a limit
to how many tokens can be fit in this

00:06:53.919 --> 00:07:00.960
context before it runs out of room
because an LLM is designed to be able to

00:06:59.039 --> 00:07:05.759
look back over a number of different
tokens as it's forming its predicted

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:10.160
next tokens. And each LLM can only look
back so far that they they're only large

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:16.400
enough to look back a certain amount of
tokens. And if you try and pass in more

00:07:12.639 --> 00:07:20.800
tokens than its maximum context window,
it will fail. That is considered a

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:25.840
break. It can't handle it and it will be
an error. It's the maximum number of

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:30.319
tokens that it can examine in its input
in order to predict the next tokens.

00:07:28.479 --> 00:07:36.400
That's called it's sometimes just called
the context window or the context window

00:07:33.199 --> 00:07:42.560
limit, but it is the maximum size of
this this context that's passed in. And

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:47.919
so it needs to fit within this context
window. And that's something which many

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:52.000
of us agonize over constantly. And
people people obsess over this this

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:55.599
context limits the the fact that that
you can't go over it. But in fact, it's

00:07:53.919 --> 00:08:00.879
worth keeping in mind that in a lot of
cases, just having too much in the

00:07:58.879 --> 00:08:06.240
context, even if you're not yet at the
limit, it can still cause performance to

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:10.400
degrade. And I'm using performance
there, not in the kind of speed sense,

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:13.759
not not performance like like we
software people sometimes say. I'm

00:08:11.919 --> 00:08:17.680
talking about the accuracy, the quality
of the results, that kind of performance

00:08:15.919 --> 00:08:23.120
that can start to degrade. You get
poorer results. You start to lose

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:27.520
coherence. It starts to forget things as
you fill up the context more and more.

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:31.680
So, even if you're not yet at the limit,
even if you haven't filled up the

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:36.399
context window, you might start to see
degrading performance if you're shoving

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:40.560
too much in the context. And conversely,
you tend to get the best performance at

00:08:38.719 --> 00:08:44.159
the very start of your conversation when
the the context is is super empty and

00:08:42.719 --> 00:08:48.240
it's only got a little bit to pay
attention to. And so, that's something

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:54.080
to be very cognizant of. It's not just a
binary, have you got too much, it fails.

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:57.600
There's also a sense of of less less is
more when it comes to filling up the

00:08:55.760 --> 00:09:01.279
context. Okay. Now, I've been saying
that the LLM fails when the context

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:06.800
window gets filled up. And you're
probably thinking, well, I I don't think

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:10.160
it fails. I think it it does compacting.
You've probably experienced compacting

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:14.560
before. That's actually a feature built
in to things like clawed code, which is

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:18.320
that rather than allowing it to fail, it
detects when the context window is

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:23.200
almost filled up and it runs this
process that essentially looks back at

00:09:20.399 --> 00:09:29.200
the whole conversation history and then
summarizes it and replaces everything

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:33.680
with a little summary of what was there
before, freeing up lots of space in the

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:37.440
context history in the in the
conversation history. And and this is

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:42.480
something which which is very common and
it's also something which is feared by

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:46.399
by people like you and me a lot because
it it changes the game suddenly. The

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:50.880
challenge with compacting is that we are
trusting the LLM to do a good job of

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:54.240
figuring out what's important and what's
not important from the conversation

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:59.200
history. And often it does a good job
but sometimes it doesn't. Sometimes it

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:02.720
misses an important thing uh that that
we care a lot about that we that we told

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:07.680
it about during the conversation that
gets removed when it's summarizing and

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:11.200
as a result uh the LLM appears to forget
something that we've told it or it makes

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:15.279
the same mistake twice which is super
frustrating and because of this people

00:10:13.279 --> 00:10:19.920
people have have tended to fear the
compactor don't like it when it compacts

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:24.399
and often you'll you'll find that people
and I I do this a lot myself rather than

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:30.000
let it compact I will look at what's
happened before I will stop the agent

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:35.120
and then I will rewrite my file like
agents.mmd that I mentioned before and

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:38.560
then start it up again fresh making sure
that I've kept careful note of

00:10:37.279 --> 00:10:43.760
everything that's happened that it needs
to know about. So people do tend to fear

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:47.600
the compacting process and like to stop
it and refresh it and rewrite it because

00:10:45.839 --> 00:10:51.519
there's a lack of trust for how
compacting works. There's a particular

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:54.959
fear as well for if it if it's going to
run compacting whilst it's in the middle

00:10:53.279 --> 00:10:58.240
of doing something. And that's something
that that you can get around by forcing

00:10:56.480 --> 00:11:02.079
it to compact at a good time. And that's
a bit a bit different. What I'm talking

00:11:00.079 --> 00:11:06.720
about is just a general fear of
compacting. But the truth of the matter

00:11:03.839 --> 00:11:13.200
is compacting has got a lot better in a
just recent history. And now as of 2026,

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:19.279
uh there is less reason to fear the
compactor. It does a really good job.

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:24.800
So, I am still of the old guard that I
do tend to still like to stop and

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:29.519
summarize my agents.mmd, but I know that
some of that is my stubbornness at this

00:11:26.959 --> 00:11:33.680
point and that often you can just trust
the compactor. And so, I'm here to tell

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:39.360
you, don't be like me. At least start
out by trusting the compactor. Trust

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:43.920
compacting. Let it do its thing. and it
will often do a really fine job of

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:49.120
summarizing the conversation history,
keeping relevant stuff that it needs to

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:53.519
remember and freeing up tons of space
for more conversation. That's what it

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:56.320
does and it tends to do it well. And
maybe one more point to mention on

00:11:54.800 --> 00:12:04.240
context windows is just to tell you the
the context window size of of different

00:11:59.279 --> 00:12:11.040
major models. So, uh OpenAI's uh GPT 5.2
two that that we used yesterday. Uh that

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:18.959
has a context window of 400,000
tokens. 400,000 tokens. Uh Claude, the

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:25.200
Claude Sonnet 45 called Opus 45 that has
200,000 tokens. So about half. And

00:12:22.639 --> 00:12:30.480
Gemini and and the Gemini in in
anti-gravity for from Google that has a

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:35.279
context window of a million tokens. A
million tokens. Much greater. So these

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:40.079
these are the limits if you it can't go
above that number. But it's again worth

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:44.959
pointing out that you'll see degrading
performance as you start to put more in

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:49.760
the context. So personally I I don't
overobsess about these max context

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:55.200
window sizes because I'm more interested
in in in how is performance affected by

00:12:52.880 --> 00:13:01.760
putting more into the context. And I
find across all the models that having

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:01.760
less in the context gets better results.

